 looked up so we're almost out of time  this hour gun has gone too fast  Howard Simon's deceased former managing  editor of the Washington Post  you write in the acknowledgement section  that he twice helped you to the  newspaper once when I was out of college  and then you say the second time when I  left the paper in a snip I've got to ask  you what that snip was I didn't get a  promotion that I thought I deserved and  so I quit and then I came crawling back  about nine months later and said I want  to go overseas please please please and  they took me back where'd you go for  those nine months I went to work for the  Washingtonians magazine in Washington  which was a good job but it wasn't being  overseas which I wanted to go there's  another interesting little note here  about his daughter Anna an  anthropologist this is Howard Simon's  daughter Anna and an anthropologist who  specializes in Africa she had some  impact on you but she's a wonderful  woman  she came to Nairobi she was going to do  work with with the red Dilley up north  pastoral people she ended up working  with the Somalis but any case she showed  me where a lot of the levers were in  African literature and short-circuited  my research for this book by six months  of enormous value in this book were a  series of long wonderful dinners in  Khartoum and Nairobi with Abdul Mohammed  a good friend and energetic thinker  who helped me understand what I was  seeing in Africa well this guy is an  Ethiopian and he was kicked out of  Ethiopia because of political  differences with the leadership but he's  a scholar and he read very widely of the  African literature and he read my  stories and he told me what was wrong  with them what he thought was wrong with  them and I learned how to conceptualize  it what you have to do in Africa it's  very hard to understand what's going on  so you have to pick a broad theme and go  out and try to fill it in  see how put human beings inside a broad  theme and he suggested various people to  look at various places to go and I miss  him one last person I want to ask you  about Robert Caputo yes he is a  photographer for National Geographic and  has been all over East Africa for the  past 15 years he took many of the  pictures for the book he travelled with  me when I did the mini ball story and he  was very enthusiastic about this book  from the beginning last question what's  the best thing about ever the best thing  about Africa is that people are warm and  they love you on that note there is a  lot more in this book and I wish we had  time to talk about it Africa by blaine  hard reporter for The Washington Post  dispatches from a fragile continent  thank you very much thank you
 

